{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_mwgbfudhd6nsgr2kljrz7p67r6ec2ke4wnhazbiilqdg7oagc52q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: the Rust translation is byte-identical to the C for every input tested. No divergences found; no source fixes to `translation/src/lib.rs` were needed.**\n\n### What was verified\n\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `cargo check` clean | ✅ (was already clean) |\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` diff empty | ✅ all 7 C exports present in Rust `.so`; no unresolvable undefined symbols |\n| Phase B — all 32 `CONFIGS.md` rows | ✅ `tests/phase_b_valid.rs`, 33 tests |\n| Phase C — all 20 `ERRORS.md` rows + generic boundaries | ✅ `tests/phase_c_errors.rs`, 21 tests |\n| Binary/driver stdout diff | n/a — crate is `cdylib` only, no `[[bin]]` |\n| All feature combos | ✅ no `[features]` table; matrix run over `{dev, release} × {default, --no-default-features, --all-features}` |\n\n### Test suite (`translation/tests/`)\nBoth libraries are loaded via `libloading` and called only through `dlsym`, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are what's under test. Because the two `.so`s share the process's glibc, they share one `stdout` — so **stdout is captured via fd-1 redirection and compared byte-for-byte alongside every return value**, which is essential here since most of this library's behaviour is `printf` output.\n\n- `common/mod.rs` — loader, fd-1 capture, `diff`/`diff_batch`, fixed-seed PRNG, and a `fork()`-based heap-exhaustion harness.\n- `phase_b_valid.rs` / `phase_c_errors.rs` / `phase_d_symbols.rs` / `bulk_sweep.rs`\n- `harness_selftest.rs` — **negative controls**, so passes aren't vacuous: proves capture records real output, that `diff`/`diff_batch`/`diff_oom` each *fail* on return-value, stdout-only, and out-parameter divergences, and that the two `.so`s are distinct objects with distinct symbol addresses.\n\n### Notable findings\n- **The `malloc`-failure rows are executed for real**, not assumed. `run_oom` forks, lowers `RLIMIT_AS`, and drains the allocator; each test first asserts the *C* side actually took the failure branch (e.g. `complexmode` → `-1` + `\"Failed to allocate result tracker\\n\"`) before comparing to Rust. All three reached it.\n- Three C branches are provably **dead** and documented as such: `safe_add`'s rejection and mode 2's empty-log path are unreachable from `complexmode`, and mode 4's `EXEC_PERM` multiply branch never runs because `0644 & 0100 == 0` — so `complexmode(4,5,7,9)` returns `21`, not `44`. The Rust matches.\n- Large *positive* `count` for `copy_and_sum` is deliberately excluded: under overcommit the C `malloc`s 8 GiB, `memcpy`s past the source array and SIGSEGVs. That's genuine UB, identical in both, and running it just kills the harness.\n- Scale: 8000 inputs/row in Phase B and ~14 M compared calls in `bulk_sweep` at `CTORUST_BULK=1000000` — all identical.\n\n### Files I added outside `tests/`\n- `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md` — Phase A artifacts, all rows checked.\n- `run_matrix.sh` — builds the C lib, enumerates feature combos, and runs check + build + `nm` diff + tests across all six configurations. Ends `ALL CONFIGURATIONS PASSED`.\n- `Cargo.toml`: added `libloading = \"0.8\"` to `[dev-dependencies]`.\n- `.cargo/config.toml`: two settings you should know about, since both change how `cargo test` behaves in this crate — `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` (mandatory: libtest's own progress output races with the fd-1 capture and produces false failures otherwise) and `net.offline = true` (crates.io is unreachable from this sandbox; `libloading` resolved from the local registry cache).\n\n`c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the given cmake command creates."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":354,"cache_read_input_tokens":141496,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":354,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b294213d-bfd6-49f5-acda-fb29088cc8b5","uuid":"e774cc9b-5b3a-4e0e-8272-5e20449b7462","timestamp":"2026-09-05T13:53:41.997Z"}
